{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.ParallelogramContour","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.ParallelogramContour","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.CauchyIntegral","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.HasPrimitives","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.FundThmCalculus","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.IntegrationByParts"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:parallelogramIntegral_inv_sub_eq_two_pi_i","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"parallelogramIntegral_inv_sub_eq_two_pi_i","kind":"axiom","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ParallelogramContour.lean#L173-L177","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:parallelogramIntegral_eq_zero_of_differentiable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"parallelogramIntegral_eq_zero_of_differentiable","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ParallelogramContour.lean#L93-L117","doc":"**Cauchy's theorem for the fundamental parallelogram (PROVED).**\n\nThe contour integral of an entire function around the boundary of the parallelogram with base point\n`z₀` and periods `ω₁, ω₂` vanishes.  See the section header for why this needs no Goursat. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:edge_integral_eq_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"edge_integral_eq_sub","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ParallelogramContour.lean#L72-L91","doc":"**One edge integrates to a difference of primitive values.**\n\n`∫₀¹ ω · f(a + tω) dt = F(a + ω) − F(a)` whenever `F` is a primitive of `f` on a ball containing\nthe segment.  The chain rule supplies `HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ => F (a + s·ω)) (ω · f (a + tω)) t`;\nthe fundamental theorem of calculus does the rest. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:segment_mem_ball","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"segment_mem_ball","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ParallelogramContour.lean#L58-L70","doc":"Every point of the segment `t ↦ a + t·ω`, `t ∈ [0,1]`, lies in the ball of radius\n`‖a‖ + ‖ω‖ + ε` about the origin. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:parallelogramIntegral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"parallelogramIntegral","kind":"def","line":12,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/ParallelogramContour.lean#L12-L18","doc":"The contour integral of `f` over the boundary of the fundamental parallelogram\nwith base point `z₀` and periods `ω₁, ω₂`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}